    {
      "id": "8ng2597",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Classification of Rivers of India"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Indian rivers are classified into two major systems: the Himalayan Rivers (perennial) and the Peninsular Rivers (seasonal/rain-fed).",
        "The major Himalayan river systems include the Indus, Ganga, and Brahmaputra, which originate from snow and glaciers.",
        "Godavari is the largest peninsular river system in India and is widely known as the 'Dakshin Ganga'.",
        "Most east-flowing rivers (like Ganga, Godavari, Krishna, Cauvery) form deltas, whereas major west-flowing rivers (like Narmada and Tapi) flow through rift valleys and form estuaries.",
        "Indian drainage systems display distinct geometric patterns such as dendritic, radial, trellis, and centripetal depending on geological structure."
      ],
      "id": "aemgswd"
    },
